{
  "schema": 1,
  "grade": "8",
  "band": "6-8",
  "focus": "Grade 8 is the year of nuance and critical reading. Students distinguish near-synonyms and implicit from explicit meaning, detect bias and rhetoric, reason ethically, weigh historical sources and scientific claims, and analyze literature with the terms critics use. The word-part work culminates in the roots cred, bene/mal and chron/temp, the belief-and-practice suffix -ism, and the prefixes contra-/counter-, circum-, retro- and pre-. Compared with grade 7 the vocabulary is more abstract, more evaluative and more specialized; compared with grade 9 it still teaches each technical term through concrete cases before students apply it across whole texts.",
  "selection": "Words were chosen because they name the thinking of high-school preparation: shades of meaning (nuance, implicit, ambivalent), bias and perspective, ethics, rhetorical appeals, evidence and source evaluation, literary devices, and the causes and costs of change. Criteria applied: academic utility across disciplines, comprehension of editorials, primary sources and literary fiction, semantic precision among close words, productive morphology with real roots, argument language, and multilingual-learner value (many words are Spanish cognates). Confusable pairs (principle, sanction, credulous/incredulous, implicit/explicit) were selected on purpose. Tier 3 terms are limited to three per disciplinary unit and are otherwise confined to literary and rhetoric units. Tone, claims, research, systems and data vocabulary stays in grade 7, and the concrete character and discussion vocabulary in grade 6. Words and word parts already owned by grades 4 and 5 (analyze, evaluate, justify, bias, credible, refute, the suffixes -ify/-ize/-ity and the prefixes anti-, post-) were not repeated. No publisher list or sequence was consulted.",
  "arc": [
    "Shades of Meaning — Nuance: reading what is implied, qualified and contradictory",
    "Whose Side Is This? — Bias and perspective: how a source shapes what you see",
    "Good Will, Ill Will — The roots bene and mal: good and bad",
    "Putting Evidence Together — Task verbs for synthesis and critique",
    "Right and Wrong — Ethics: duty, conscience and moral choice",
    "Reading the Rhetoric — Rhetoric: how speakers persuade through character, emotion and logic",
    "Sources of the Past — History: how historians know what they know",
    "Belief, Practice and -ism — The suffix -ism: beliefs, movements and habits of mind",
    "Who Am I? — Identity and belonging: fitting in, standing apart",
    "Reading Between the Lines — Literary analysis: irony, allusion and other devices",
    "Faith in Words — The root cred: believing and trusting",
    "Dissent and Resistance — Protest, power and resistance in history",
    "How We Know — Science: theory, evidence and the limits of certainty",
    "Technology and Its Costs — Technology and society: convenience, privacy and consent",
    "Time and Order — The roots chron and temp: time",
    "Strengthening an Argument — Rhetoric: rebuttal, qualification and the strongest case",
    "The Planet's Ledger — Environment: resources, costs and stewardship",
    "Against, Around, Backward, Before — Prefixes contra-/counter-, circum-, retro- and pre-",
    "Characters Under Glass — Literary analysis: complex characters and turning points",
    "Progress and Its Price — Consequences: how solutions create new problems"
  ],
